======================================================================================
A. C005 — the hybrid score mean, computed from the definition
======================================================================================
  Definition (m1-certificate.json):  B_kappa(v) = kappa * sum_{mu in Lambda} f(v - mu).
  For a periodic packing with k centres per cell of volume V (centre density c = k/V):
      mean_v B_kappa = (kappa/V) * sum_j int_cell sum_{x in L} f(v - x - v_j) dv
                     = (kappa/V) * sum_j int_{R^3} f  =  kappa * (k/V) * fhat(0) = kappa * c * fhat(0).
  (unfolding sum_{x in L} over the cell is exact for f in L^1; no Voronoi geometry enters.)
  [PASS] mean B_kappa > 0 for kappa>0, fhat(0)>0  => C005(ii) 'mean <= 0' is REFUTED kappa*c*fhat(0) = 5.568328*c
  [PASS] FCC separation 2: centre density c = 1/(4 sqrt2)                 c = 0.176776695
     so for FCC the mean is kappa*c*fhat(0) = kappa*fhat(0)/(4 sqrt2) — the SAME constant
     C005(i) quoted as a lower bound for B_kappa(ico). The 'opposite signs' contrast is void.

  Distant centres: is B_kappa(v0) = kappa(f(0)+12 f(2)) correct for an infinite packing?
     a= 1.0 : f(2)/f(0) = 1.832e-02 ; contact term is 2.2e-01 of the centre term
     a= 3.0 : f(2)/f(0) = 6.144e-06 ; contact term is 7.4e-05 of the centre term
     a=10.0 : f(2)/f(0) = 4.248e-18 ; contact term is 5.1e-17 of the centre term
     (a) the 12-contact formula is exact only if f vanishes beyond the second shell
         (r2 = 2.102924 > 2 for this cluster), i.e. only for compactly supported f.
     (b) for such f, f(2) is a boundary value of the support, so the contact term is
         not independently normalisable; for unbounded support the tail sum is positive.
  [PASS] icosahedral cluster second-shell radius > 2                      r2 = 2.102924

