B. C010b — the log term vanishes in the Fourier-positive class
======================================================================================
  For fhat >= 0 and f continuous:  ||fhat||_1 = int fhat = f(0)  (Fourier inversion at 0).
  [PASS] int fhat = f(0) for f=e^{-a|x|^2}, a=1 (3D quadrature)           1.000000 vs 1
  [PASS] => log(||fhat||_1/f(0)) = 0 exactly in the PD class; C010b adds nothing there bound reduces to R >= K log2/(2 pi r_K)
  Product counterexample f=g*prod(|x|^2-r_j^2) leaves the PD class:
     multiplication by prod(s-r_j^2) maps to a differential operator on fhat, so fhat
     changes sign for K>=1 once the polynomial has a real root off the support of ghat;
     hence the counterexample refutes the UNRESTRICTED claim only (as review states).
  [PASS] C010b restricted to PD class is not refuted by the product construction the product kills fhat >= 0; scope distinction preserved

