======================================================================================
D. Tammes-13 and the varying-radius reduction (explicit delta_0)
======================================================================================
  Setup: y_1..y_13 with |y_i| in [2,2+delta] and |y_i-y_j| >= 2. Project y_i' = y_i/|y_i|.
  For radii u,v in [2,2+delta],  |y_i-y_j|^2 = u^2+v^2-2uv cos(theta) >= 4  gives
      cos(theta) <= (u^2+v^2-4)/(2uv), maximised at u=v=2+delta, i.e. 1 - 2/(2+delta)^2.
  So all pairwise angles of the 13 projected points exceed alpha(delta)=arccos(1-2/(2+delta)^2).
  Musin-Tarasov (arXiv:1002.1439, DCG 48 (2012) 128-141): d13 = 57.1367... deg is the
  maximum possible minimal angle for 13 points. Contradiction whenever alpha(delta) > d13.
     alpha(delta) > 60 deg at delta=0 (kissing number 12 = Schuette-van der Waerden)
     delta_max(d13 = 57.1367 deg) = 0.091146
     delta_max(d13 <= 57.14 deg)  = 0.091035   <- conservative certificate
  [PASS] explicit tolerance: N(rho)=12 for 2 <= rho <= 2+delta_0 with delta_0 = 0.091 delta_0 >= 0.091035 (conservative)
  [PASS] the required Tammes input is d13 < 60 deg (equivalently: kissing number 12) 57.14 deg < 60 deg
     NOTE: this converts C013's 'open delta_0' into a proved explicit constant, at the
     price of citing Musin-Tarasov. It is not new mathematics: the reduction is elementary.

